TIONAL: when the proof was made, as Data Integrity defines it. Covered by the proof; the statement\'s own `iat` is what the mediator checks."},"proofValue":{"type":"string","minLength":2,"maxLength":4096,"pattern":"^z[1-9A-HJ-NP-Za-km-z]+\$","description":"The multibase (base58btc) signature."}}},"StatementScope":{"title":"StatementScope","type":"array","maxItems":16,"items":{"\$ref":"#/\$defs/MuxScopeValue"},"description":"The scope a member DID signs into a statement: a JSON array of MuxScopeValue, compared as a set — order is not significant and a repeated value collapses. It MUST NOT be empty; an empty signed scope is refused with the task\'s `emptyScope` code, never read as \\"all\\". Unlike MuxScope, emptiness is not a schema failure here, so that the refusal is reported per statement rather than failing the whole batch."},"MuxScopeValue":{"title":"MuxScopeValue","type":"string","enum":["receive","pickup","delete","send"],"description":"One capability a member DID extends to a principal\'s connection. `receive`: live delivery of the member\'s inbound messages on the connection. `pickup`: message-pickup status and delivery requests naming the member as `recipient_did`, and inclusion in a raw-TSP inbox drain. `delete`: deleting the member\'s stored messages, including delete-on-ack and delete-on-send; a member without `delete` keeps its messages in its inbox until something holding `delete` collects them. `send`: the mediator accepts on this connection frames whose sender is the member DID — the frame is still signed or authcrypted with the member\'s own keys; the scope only lets the mediator accept it on this socket."}}}';

